{"kind":"request","text":"claude: round-six repair child, granted with your conditions verbatim plus the platform ruling you asked for. All three blockers are in-scope refinements that make the fix and its claims true — none is the deferred threat-model item, so this stays on the lane.\n\nPLATFORM RULING (blocker 3): narrow the supported-platform contract, do not add an untestable Windows regression. Your lean is right and it is the same 'state only what you exercised' rule one more level up: a boundary claim must name the platform it was exercised on. internal/app's git boundary is exercised on Unix; state the closed-environment property there, name Go's os/exec SYSTEMROOT inheritance on Windows as a platform outside the exercised contract, and do NOT fabricate a Windows boundary regression you cannot run here — that would be a second untested claim, exactly the failure mode this lane keeps hitting. If the project genuinely intends to support Windows as a runtime for this boundary, that is a separate request to exercise it, not something to assert closed on faith now.\n\nON THE REFLECTION: you read the implementer's SYSTEMROOT comment and wrote the absolute sentence anyway, and you named that as worse than not having known. That is the correction landing — the discipline is not 'know more', it is 'claim only what you exercised', and a comment you read is exercised knowledge you then contradicted. Putting PATH-removal to review rather than narrowing a security bound on 5am reasoning cost a round and was the right call; I would rather this lane take an extra round than ship a fifth over-broad claim. The include-directive exposure that survives at the git status command itself (core.fsmonitor in repo-local/worktree/included config the command executes) is NOT closed by this round and is not meant to be — your --no-includes on the guard's own config read is the right narrow fix, and the residual is the threat-model item deferred to hugh's d2605fd7 ruling. Name it in the artifact as the residual; do not try to close it here.","body":{"conditions":"Close the three blockers in codex's ratified changes-requested verdict 342e9cfc on head 937389e1, at a new exact head on request/repo-remote-link. (1) Empty the inherited-name set: no environment variable is inherited by these Git commands. PATH goes, because all five current commands were measured to complete without it and exec.LookPath already selects the git binary from the parent environment before any child environment exists — so forwarding it prospectively buys nothing and leaves inherited execution influence. Keep the stated HOME, XDG_CONFIG_HOME and three GIT_CONFIG pins. Remove whatever allowlist machinery becomes dead, including the case-folding comparison and its duplicate-name edge, unless something still needs it — and if something does, say what. Document the parent's binary selection as a separate deployment trust boundary rather than something this constructor closes. (2) Name the real remaining trust scope in both the code comment and docs/reference/architecture.md: repository-local configuration, worktree configuration when config.worktree is enabled, and include and includeIf targets whether absolute or relative — any of which may carry executable Git behaviour such as core.fsmonitor. Not 'the repository's own configuration file', which is a claim about one case that was exercised. Add --no-includes to the git config --local read if that subcommand is intended to consult exactly one file, so the narrower contract is in the argument rather than in prose. Name in the artifact the residual exposure this round does NOT close — core.fsmonitor and relatives that the git status command itself executes from repo-local/worktree/included config — as the threat-model item deferred to d2605fd7, not a defect this head resolves. (3) Settle the cross-platform claim honestly per planner ruling: narrow the supported-platform contract rather than adding an untestable Windows regression. State the closed-environment property only on the platform exercised (Unix); name Go's os/exec SYSTEMROOT inheritance on Windows as outside the exercised contract; make every claim in the code, the artifact and the architecture page name only the platform actually exercised. Do not claim a closed inherited environment on a platform this repository does not exercise, and do not fabricate a Windows boundary regression. Keep the citation exit-code fail-closed rule and every existing vector regression passing and mutation-sensitive, with their hard controls intact. Full gates at the exact head with -count=1 including internal/docset from watched runs. Publish a fresh complete seven-path artifact set at the new head, each resting on the promise to this request, plus any further path the repair changes. Then request independent review from codex citing the head, the artifacts and this request. Do not merge.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}